1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,132
The Leaning Tower of Pisa should have
fallen over more than 800 years ago,

2
00:00:05,132 --> 00:00:07,145
when it was only three floors tall,

3
00:00:07,145 --> 00:00:10,366
and what saved it wasn't some genius
engineer but a war.

4
00:00:10,816 --> 00:00:14,674
So to see how a war could save a tower, we
have to look underground.

5
00:00:15,124 --> 00:00:19,213
In 1173, Pisa began building on soft, wet
clay,

6
00:00:19,213 --> 00:00:23,431
with a foundation just three meters deep,
and by the third floor,

7
00:00:23,431 --> 00:00:25,313
one side was already sinking.

8
00:00:25,763 --> 00:00:29,397
Then Pisa went to war, and the work
stopped for almost a century,

9
00:00:29,397 --> 00:00:33,981
while the tower's weight slowly squeezed
water out of the clay and made it firmer,

10
00:00:33,981 --> 00:00:37,168
because if they'd kept building, it would
have collapsed.

11
00:00:37,618 --> 00:00:39,236
When the builders came back,

12
00:00:39,236 --> 00:00:43,282
they made each new floor a little taller
on one side to hide the lean,

13
00:00:43,282 --> 00:00:46,807
so the tower isn't straight at all but
curved, like a banana.

14
00:00:47,257 --> 00:00:51,027
By 1990 it was leaning so far that it had
to close,

15
00:00:51,027 --> 00:00:56,865
until engineers pulled soil from under the
high side and straightened it by almost
half a meter.

16
00:00:57,315 --> 00:01:01,575
And that same soft clay has saved it from
at least four strong earthquakes,

17
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:04,074
by keeping it from swaying with the
shaking,

18
00:01:04,074 --> 00:01:07,823
so the ground that made it lean is the
reason it's still standing.
